--[==[
JSON encoding supports typed [](serde.Binding) values and ordinary tables.
Use [](encode) and [](decode) for bindings, or [](encodevalue) and
[](decodevalue) for schema-free values.

```nupp
local json = serde.json
local record Order
    id: integer
    items: {string}
    note: string?
end
local orders = serde.binding(Order)
local bytes = json.encode(orders, Order(id = 9, items = {"tea"}))
assert(bytes == '{"id":9,"items":["tea"],"note":null}')
local order = json.decode(orders, bytes)
assert(order.items[1] == "tea" and order.note == nil)
```

What a binding needs to be written is worked out on first use and kept, so the
second call with a binding does none of that work again. Native AOT builds also
select a compiled decoder for bindings whose carriers it supports.

The module-level functions share one [](Codec) with [](defaultpolicy), which
reads declaration bindings and documents. A model library passes a
[](PolicyFactory) of its own to [](codec), or [](membernamepolicy) when its
member names are already the wire names.

Decoding is strict: a declaration binding rejects unknown members unless its
[](serde.Options) say `unknownmembers = "ignore"`, integers must fit their
declared width, and nesting and token counts are bounded. Every failure is a
[](serde.Error) naming the path and byte offset where it happened.

## Empty containers

A plain empty Lua table has no shape to read, so it encodes as `{}`. Mark it
when it must encode as an array, or use the two sentinels:

```nupp
const json = serde.json

assert(json.encodevalue({}) == "{}")
assert(json.encodevalue(json.asarray({})) == "[]")
assert(json.encodevalue(json.EMPTY_ARRAY) == "[]")
assert(json.encodevalue(json.EMPTY_OBJECT) == "{}")

local emptyArray = assert(json.decodevalue("[]"))
assert(json.isarray(emptyArray))
local emptyObject = assert(json.decodevalue("{}"))
assert(not json.isarray(emptyObject))
```

`asobject` is the corresponding mark for a table that must encode as an object.
`isarray` reads the shape retained by decoded containers, including when they
are empty.

## JSON null

Decoding drops JSON null by default, including from inside an array. Pass a
replacement value to preserve it. `NULL` is the round-trippable choice, because
it encodes back as null:

```nupp:fragment
const json = serde.json

local value = assert(json.decodevalue([[{"items":[1,null,2]}]], json.NULL))
assert(value.items[2] == json.NULL)
assert(json.encodevalue(value) == [[{"items":[1,null,2]}]])
```

## Pulling selected values

`pull` validates the complete document while applying its selection. `true`
selects a complete value, an object shape selects named fields, and
`arrayof(shape)` applies a shape to every member of an array. Missing and
unselected fields are omitted:

```nupp
const json = serde.json

local source = '[{"id": 1, "role": "admin", "profile": {"name": "Ada", "age": 36}}]'
local users, reason = json.pull(source, json.arrayof({id = true, profile = {name = true}}))
```

This is the lower-level path for pull deserializers: it validates the complete
input but constructs only the Lua values the shape asks for.

## Streaming output

The `own.Closeable` writer emits checked JSON into caller-owned storage without first
building a Lua table or a complete result string. `endarray()` and
`endobject()` end containers, `flush()` publishes a bounded staged batch while
leaving the document open, and consuming `close()` requires one complete root:

```nupp
local out = io.Buffer.create()
local writer = serde.json.Writer.of(out)
writer:startobject():key("items"):startarray():write(1):write(2):endarray():endobject()
writer:close()
```

Lexical destruction closes a live writer automatically. The public writer
identity is single-use. Closing publishes its final staged parts and leaves
that identity closed; untyped stale references therefore cannot affect a later
document.

A prepared `serde` JSON traversal can write at the current value
position. Its recursive walk drains completed fields and list members at
the same threshold, so a large root does not need to exist as one staged string.

`encoded(value)` performs ordinary encoding once. `verified(text)` instead
validates an existing immutable JSON string without decoding or re-encoding it.
Both return a value that `write` appends without another walk or validation.
`encodedstring(value)` and `verifiedstring(text)` provide the corresponding
string-only form accepted by both `key` and `write`; these handles are interned,
so repeated schema keys share their encoded representation.
]==]
module serde.json

local core = require("nupp.serde.internal.value")
local publication = require("nupp.serde.internal.publication")
local plans = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.plan")
local readers = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.reader")
local writers = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.writer")
local documents = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.document")
local syntax = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.syntax")
local json = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.values")
const {type Buffer} = require("nupp.io.internal.bytes")
local contexts = require("nupp.serde.internal.context")
local errors = require("nupp.serde.internal.errors")
local extensions = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.extensions")
local compiled = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.compiled")
local lowering = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.lower")
local defaults = require("nupp.serde.internal.json.defaults")
local documentValues = require("nupp.serde.internal.document")

--- Identifies JSON as the codec in a [](serde.Context) it attaches, so a
--- protocol can tell a document it read from one another codec read.
export const identity: table = {}

local interface WriteOperation<T>
    encode: function(self, borrows value: T): string
    append: function(self, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: Buffer): nil
    writeValue: function(self, borrows value: T, exclusive sink: json.Writer): nil
end
local record Write<T, M is core.Member> is WriteOperation<T>
    @readonly adapter: core.Adapter<T, M>
    @readonly factory: plans.PolicyFactory
    @readonly operation: extensions.Operation<T, M>
    @readonly cache: extensions.Scope
    function writeValue(self, borrows value: T, exclusive sink: json.Writer): nil
        local operation = self.operation
        local encoding = operation.encoding
        if encoding ~= nil then
            local ok, problem = pcall(
                encoding.write,
                encoding,
                value,
                sink,
                encoding.dynamic and lowering.Budget() or nil
            )
            if not ok then
                sink:abort()
                error(errors.wrap(problem, "$", nil), 0)
            end
            return
        end
        local writer = writers.output(sink, self.factory, operation.policy, operation.plan, self.cache)
        writer:emit(self.adapter, value)
        writer:close()
    end

    function append(self, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: Buffer): nil
        local sink = json.newWriter(buffer)
        self:writeValue(value, sink)
        sink:close()
    end

    function encode(self, borrows value: T): string
        local buffer = require("nupp.io.internal.bytes").newBuffer()
        self:append(value, buffer)
        return buffer:tostring()
    end
end

-- Writes a value straight into a caller's byte buffer in the writer's batches.
local function appendTo<T>(selected: WriteOperation<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: io.Buffer): nil
    const sink = json.newBufferWriter(buffer)
    selected:writeValue(value, sink)
    sink:close()
end

local record SelectWrite<T> is core.Consumer<T, nil, nil>
    @readonly factory: plans.PolicyFactory
    @readonly cache: extensions.Scope
    result: WriteOperation<T>?
    function apply<M is core.Member>(self, adapter: core.Adapter<T, M>, borrows state: nil): nil
        self.result = Write<T, M>(
            adapter = adapter,
            factory = self.factory,
            operation = self.cache:resolve(adapter, "write"),
            cache = self.cache
        )
    end
end

local interface ReadOperation<T>
    read: function(self, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): T
end
local record Read<T, M is core.Member> is ReadOperation<T>
    @readonly binding: core.Binding<T>
    @readonly native: compiled.Decoder<T>?
    @readonly adapter: core.Adapter<T, M>
    @readonly factory: plans.PolicyFactory
    @readonly operation: extensions.Operation<T, M>
    function read(self, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): T
        local operation = self.operation
        local native = self.native
        if native ~= nil and native:accepts(bytes) then
            return native:decode(bytes)
        end
        local cursor = syntax.reader(bytes:sub(1))
        local source = contexts.builder(identity, self.factory as table, context):freeze()
        local reader = readers.input(cursor, operation.policy, operation.plan, source)
        local ok, result = pcall(self.adapter.read, self.adapter, reader)
        reader:expire()
        if not ok then
            error(errors.wrap(result, cursor:path(), cursor:offset()), 0)
        end
        local complete, problem = pcall(cursor.finish, cursor)
        if not complete then
            error(publication.failure(self.binding, result as T, problem), 0)
        end

        return result as T
    end
end
local record SelectRead<T> is core.Consumer<T, nil, nil>
    @readonly binding: core.Binding<T>
    @readonly factory: plans.PolicyFactory
    @readonly cache: extensions.Scope
    result: ReadOperation<T>?
    function apply<M is core.Member>(self, adapter: core.Adapter<T, M>, borrows state: nil): nil
        local operation = self.cache:resolve(adapter, "read")
        self.result = Read<T, M>(
            binding = self.binding,
            native = getmetatable(self.binding) == core.Bound and operation.native or nil,
            adapter = adapter,
            factory = self.factory,
            operation = operation
        )
    end
end

local function decodeContents<D>(
    adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>,
    bytes: string,
    factory: plans.PolicyFactory,
    context: core.Context?
): D
    local cursor = syntax.reader(bytes)
    local source = contexts.builder(identity, factory as table, context):freeze()
    local reader = documents.contents(cursor, source)
    local ok, result = pcall(adapter.readcontents, adapter, reader)
    reader:expire()
    if not ok then
        error(errors.wrap(result, cursor:path(), cursor:offset()), 0)
    end
    local complete, problem = pcall(cursor.finish, cursor)
    if not complete then
        error(publication.failure(adapter, result as D, problem), 0)
    end

    return result as D
end

--- A JSON codec with its own policy and its own cache of what it selected for
--- each binding.
---
--- The module-level functions use one shared codec with [](defaultpolicy). Hold
--- a codec of your own to use another policy, or to bound and clear what it
--- retains.
export interface Codec
    --- Encodes a document's contents. See [](encodedocument).
    encodedocument: function<D>(self, adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, borrows value: D): string

    --- Decodes document contents. See [](decodedocument).
    decodedocument: function<D>(self, adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): D

    --- Writes one value into a JSON writer. See [](writevalue).
    writevalue: function<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive sink: json.Writer): nil

    --- Encodes a value. See [](encode).
    encode: function<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T): string

    --- Appends a value to a buffer. See [](encodeinto).
    encodeinto: function<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: io.Buffer): nil

    --- Decodes a value. See [](decode).
    decode: function<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): T

    --- Decodes a value from a buffer. See [](decodebuffer).
    decodebuffer: function<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, exclusive input: io.Buffer, context: core.Context?): T

    --- Forgets every binding's selections.
    clear: function(self): nil

    --- Returns how many bindings have selections retained.
    cachedbindings: function(self): integer
end

local record Cached is Codec
    @readonly factory: plans.PolicyFactory
    @readonly cache: extensions.Scope
    lastReadBinding: unknown = nil
    lastRead: unknown = nil
    lastWrite: unknown = nil

    function observeBinding(self, binding: unknown): nil
        if self.lastReadBinding ~= binding then
            self.lastReadBinding, self.lastRead, self.lastWrite = binding, nil, nil
        end
    end

    function reading<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>): ReadOperation<T>
        self:observeBinding(binding)
        local selected: ReadOperation<T>
        if self.lastRead ~= nil then
            -- Binding identity fixes T; execution captures neither input nor
            -- call context. A different binding drops this one-entry cache.
            selected = self.lastRead as ReadOperation<T>
        else
            local consumer = SelectRead<T>(binding = binding, factory = self.factory, cache = self.cache)
            binding:accept(consumer, nil)
            selected = assert(consumer.result, "binding did not supply its adapter")
            self.lastReadBinding, self.lastRead = binding, selected
        end

        return selected
    end

    function writing<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>): WriteOperation<T>
        self:observeBinding(binding)
        if self.lastWrite == nil then
            local consumer = SelectWrite<T>(factory = self.factory, cache = self.cache)
            binding:accept(consumer, nil)
            self.lastWrite = assert(consumer.result, "binding did not supply its adapter")
        end

        return self.lastWrite as WriteOperation<T>
    end

    function encodedocument<D>(self, adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, borrows value: D): string
        return self:encode(adapter:contents(), value)
    end

    function decodedocument<D>(self, adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): D
        return decodeContents(adapter, bytes, self.factory, context)
    end

    function writevalue<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive sink: json.Writer): nil
        self:writing(binding):writeValue(value, sink)
    end

    function encode<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T): string
        return self:writing(binding):encode(value)
    end

    function encodeinto<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: io.Buffer): nil
        const mark = buffer:len()
        const ok, problem = pcall(appendTo, self:writing(binding), value, buffer)
        if not ok then
            -- Batches may already have landed; cut back to what the buffer held.
            buffer:resize(mark)
            error(problem, 0)
        end
    end

    function decode<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): T
        return self:reading(binding):read(bytes, context)
    end

    function decodebuffer<T>(self, binding: core.Binding<T>, exclusive input: io.Buffer, context: core.Context?): T
        return self:reading(binding):read(input:tostring(), context)
    end

    function clear(self): nil
        self.cache:clear()
        self.lastReadBinding, self.lastRead, self.lastWrite = nil, nil, nil
    end

    function cachedbindings(self): integer
        return self.cache:size()
    end
end

--- Chooses each member's JSON name, whether it is written, and how its
--- timestamps are represented.
---
--- A model library implements one to interpret the traits on its own members,
--- such as a Smithy `jsonName` or `timestampFormat`.
export type Policy<M is core.Member> = plans.Policy<M>

--- Returns the [](Policy) for a binding's member type.
export type PolicyFactory = plans.PolicyFactory

--- Whether a policy writes a member, leaves it out, or writes it redacted.
export type Selection = plans.Selection

--- The policy for declaration bindings and documents, which the module-level
--- functions use. A model's own factory can delegate to it for members it does
--- not own.
export const defaultpolicy: plans.PolicyFactory = defaults.factory

--- The policy that writes every member under its own `name`, traverses every
--- member, and writes timestamps as RFC 3339 strings.
---
--- [](defaultpolicy) refuses a model's own members rather than guess at the
--- traits they carry, so a model library has to say how its members are named.
--- Use it for a model whose names are already the wire names.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local adapters = serde.adapters
--- local json = serde.json
--- local record Member is serde.Member
---     @readonly name: string
---     @readonly index: integer?
--- end
--- local title: serde.Binding<string> = serde.Bound<string, Member>(
---     adapter = adapters.text(Member(name = "title"))
--- )
--- assert(not pcall(json.encode, title, "Dune"))
--- local plain = json.codec(json.membernamepolicy)
--- assert(plain:encode(title, "Dune") == '"Dune"')
--- ```
export const membernamepolicy: plans.PolicyFactory = documentValues.memberNames

--- Returns the policy JSON applies inside a document-valued member, or nil when
--- the member is not one of a document's own.
---
--- A model's [](PolicyFactory) asks this first, so documents nested in its
--- values are read and written the way [](defaultpolicy) reads them.
---
--- @param member the member a codec asks about
--- @param epochtimestamps whether document timestamps are written as epoch seconds
--- @return the policy, or nil
export function documentpolicy<M is core.Member>(member: M, epochtimestamps: boolean): plans.Policy<M>?
    return documentValues.policy(member, epochtimestamps)
end

--- The wire field a JSON decoder reports for a member the model does not
--- declare.
export type FieldName = documents.Name

export const FieldName = documents.Name

--- Returns the JSON member name of a wire field.
---
--- @param field a wire field from a JSON decoder
--- @return the name
export const fieldname = documents.name

--- Makes a [](Codec).
---
--- ```nupp
--- local json = serde.json
--- local record Item
---     id: integer
--- end
--- local codec = json.codec(json.defaultpolicy, 16)
--- local items = serde.binding(Item)
--- assert(codec:decode(items, codec:encode(items, Item(id = 3))).id == 3)
--- assert(codec:cachedbindings() == 1)
--- codec:clear()
--- ```
---
--- @param policy the policy; nil is [](defaultpolicy)
--- @param capacity the most bindings to retain selections for; nil is 256
--- @return the codec
export function codec(policy: plans.PolicyFactory?, capacity: integer?): Codec
    local selected = policy or defaults.factory
    return Cached(factory = selected, cache = extensions.scope(selected, capacity))
end

local shared = codec()

--- Encodes a value as JSON text.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local json = serde.json
--- local record Pair
---     left: string
---     right: string?
--- end
--- assert(json.encode(serde.binding(Pair), Pair(left = "a")) == '{"left":"a","right":null}')
--- ```
---
--- @param binding the value's binding
--- @param value the value
--- @return the JSON text
--- @raises serde.Error when a value cannot be represented, at its path
export function encode<T>(binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T): string
    return shared:encode(binding, value)
end

--- Appends a value's JSON text to an [](io.Buffer).
---
--- On failure the buffer is left as it was.
---
--- @param binding the value's binding
--- @param value the value
--- @param buffer the buffer to append to
--- @raises serde.Error when a value cannot be represented, at its path
export function encodeinto<T>(binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive buffer: io.Buffer): nil
    shared:encodeinto(binding, value, buffer)
end

--- Decodes JSON text into a value.
---
--- The whole input must be one value; trailing text is an error. A decoded
--- value the codec then has to reject is dropped through the binding.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local json = serde.json
--- local record Range
---     low: integer
---     high: integer = 100
--- end
--- local range = json.decode(serde.binding(Range), '{"low":5}')
--- assert(range.low == 5 and range.high == 100)
--- ```
---
--- @param binding the binding to construct through
--- @param bytes the JSON text
--- @param context a parent for the context the decoder attaches, or nil
--- @return the value
--- @raises serde.Error with the path and byte offset of the failure
export function decode<T>(binding: core.Binding<T>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): T
    return shared:decode(binding, bytes, context)
end

--- Decodes the JSON text an [](io.Buffer) holds.
---
--- @param binding the binding to construct through
--- @param input the buffer holding the JSON text
--- @param context a parent for the context the decoder attaches, or nil
--- @return the value
--- @raises serde.Error with the path and byte offset of the failure
export function decodebuffer<T>(binding: core.Binding<T>, exclusive input: io.Buffer, context: core.Context?): T
    return shared:decodebuffer(binding, input, context)
end

--- Writes one value at the current position of a [](serde.json.Writer),
--- for embedding bound values in JSON assembled by hand.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local json = serde.json
--- local record Tag
---     name: string
--- end
--- local buffer = io.Buffer.create()
--- local writer = json.Writer.of(buffer)
--- writer:startarray()
--- json.writevalue(serde.binding(Tag), Tag(name = "x"), writer)
--- writer:endarray()
--- writer:close()
--- assert(buffer:tostring() == '[{"name":"x"}]')
--- ```
---
--- @param binding the value's binding
--- @param value the value
--- @param sink the writer, positioned where a value may go
--- @raises serde.Error when a value cannot be represented, at its path
export function writevalue<T>(binding: core.Binding<T>, borrows value: T, exclusive sink: json.Writer): nil
    shared:writevalue(binding, value, sink)
end

--- Encodes a document's contents as JSON text.
---
--- @param adapter the document adapter, such as [](serde.document.adapter)
--- @param value the document
--- @return the JSON text
--- @raises serde.Error when a value has no JSON representation
export function encodedocument<D>(adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, borrows value: D): string
    return shared:encodedocument(adapter, value)
end

--- Decodes JSON text as document contents, attaching the context it was read
--- under.
---
--- @param adapter the document adapter, such as [](serde.document.adapter)
--- @param bytes the JSON text
--- @param context a parent for the attached context, or nil
--- @return the document
--- @raises serde.Error with the path and byte offset of the failure
export function decodedocument<D>(adapter: core.DocumentAdapter<D>, bytes: string, context: core.Context?): D
    return shared:decodedocument(adapter, bytes, context)
end

--- A complete JSON value whose immutable bytes have already been encoded or
--- validated.
---
--- Values come from `encoded` or `verified`; the private token prevents checked
--- callers from manufacturing an unverified fast-path value.
--- @export
export type EncodedValue = json.EncodedValue

--- A JSON string whose immutable encoded bytes have already been produced or
--- validated.
---
--- `Writer:key` accepts this type without validating UTF-8 or escaping it again.
--- Values produced by `encodedstring` and `verifiedstring` are interned.
--- @export
export type EncodedString = json.EncodedString

--- Values that can write a complete JSON value through a checked writer.
---
--- A handwritten record satisfies it with a `writejson` member:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local record Money is serde.json.JSONEncodable
---     cents: integer
---
---     function writejson(self, exclusive out: serde.json.Writer): nil
---         out:startobject():key("cents"):write(self.cents):endobject()
---     end
--- end
--- ```
--- @export
export type JSONEncodable = json.JSONEncodable

--- A checked incremental JSON writer, created by `Writer.of`.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local out = io.Buffer.create()
--- local writer = serde.json.Writer.of(out)
--- writer:startobject():key("ok"):write(true):endobject()
--- writer:close()
--- assert(out:tostring() == [[{"ok":true}]])
--- ```
--- @export
export interface Writer is json.Writer
    --- Adapts caller-owned output storage for incremental JSON writing.
    ---
    --- ```nupp
    --- local out = io.Buffer.create()
    --- local writer = serde.json.Writer.of(out)
    --- writer:write(true)
    --- writer:close()
    --- assert(out:tostring() == "true")
    --- ```
    --- @param out the output storage borrowed until the writer closes
    --- @param nullvalue the value to encode as JSON null
    --- @return the incremental JSON writer
    function of(exclusive out: io.Buffer, nullvalue: any?): affine(Writer) borrows (out)
        return json.newBufferWriter(out, nullvalue)
    end
end

--- Decodes a complete JSON document.
---
--- Null members are dropped unless a replacement value is supplied. Text that is
--- not one JSON document is an ordinary answer: the result is nil and the second
--- one says why. The reason, not the value, is what tells failure apart, since
--- the document `null` decodes to nil when nulls are dropped.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local value, reason = serde.json.decodevalue([[{"id":41}]])
--- assert(reason == nil and value.id == 41)
--- local broken, why = serde.json.decodevalue([[{"id":]])
--- assert(broken == nil and why ~= nil)
--- ```
--- @param text the complete JSON document
--- @param nullValue the value used for JSON null, or nil to drop nulls
--- @return the decoded Lua value, or nil when the text is not one document
--- @return why the text is not one JSON document, when it is not
--- @export
export const decodevalue = json.decode

--- Materializes only the fields selected by an On-Demand pull shape.
---
--- The whole document is still validated. Text that is not one JSON document,
--- or a value the shape cannot select from, returns nil and a reason, as
--- `decodevalue` does.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local value = assert(serde.json.pull(
---     [[{"id":41,"profile":{"name":"Ada","admin":true}}]],
---     {id = true, profile = {name = true}}
--- ))
--- assert(value.id == 41 and value.profile.name == "Ada")
--- assert(value.profile.admin == nil)
--- ```
--- @param text the complete JSON document
--- @param shape the fields and array members to materialize
--- @param nullValue the value used for JSON null, or nil to drop nulls
--- @return the selected Lua value, or nil when the text cannot be pulled
--- @return why the text cannot be pulled, when it cannot
--- @export
export const pull = json.pull

--- Applies a pull shape to every member of an array.
---
--- ```nupp
--- const json = serde.json
--- local users = assert(json.pull(
---     [[{"users":[{"id":1,"name":"Ada"},{"id":2,"name":"Lin"}]}]],
---     {users = json.arrayof({id = true})}
--- ))
--- assert(users.users[1].id == 1 and users.users[1].name == nil)
--- assert(users.users[2].id == 2)
--- ```
--- @param shape the selection applied to each member, or every value when omitted
--- @return an array pull shape
--- @export
export const arrayof = json.arrayOf

--- Marks a Lua table as a JSON array, including while it is empty.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local values = serde.json.asarray({})
--- assert(serde.json.encodevalue(values) == "[]")
--- ```
--- @param value the table to mark
--- @return the same table
--- @export
export const asarray = json.asArray

--- Marks a Lua table as a JSON object.
---
--- ```nupp
--- const json = serde.json
--- local object = json.asobject(json.asarray({}))
--- assert(json.encodevalue(object) == "{}")
--- ```
--- @param value the table to mark
--- @return the same table
--- @export
export const asobject = json.asObject

--- Returns whether a value has JSON array shape.
---
--- Decoding retains container shape even when an array is empty. This predicate
--- reads that shape without exposing the private marker used by the codec.
---
--- ```nupp
--- const json = serde.json
--- local array = assert(json.decodevalue("[]"))
--- local object = assert(json.decodevalue("{}"))
--- assert(json.isarray(array))
--- assert(not json.isarray(object))
--- assert(json.isarray({1, 2}))
--- ```
--- @param value the value whose JSON container shape to inspect
--- @return true for a JSON array and false for an object or scalar
--- @raises string if a table mixes object and array keys or contains array holes
--- @export
export const isarray = json.isArray

--- Serializes one Lua value as JSON.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local text = serde.json.encodevalue({answer = 42})
--- assert(text == [[{"answer":42}]])
--- ```
--- @param value the value to serialize
--- @param nullValue an additional value treated as JSON null
--- @return the JSON document
--- @export
export const encodevalue = json.encode

--- Encodes a Lua value once for trusted raw insertion by a `Writer`.
---
--- The returned handle retains immutable encoded bytes. Each later write copies
--- those bytes directly to its destination without walking or validating the value.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local cached = serde.json.encoded({1, 2, 3})
--- ```
--- @param value the value to encode
--- @param nullValue an additional value treated as JSON null
--- @return the reusable encoded value
--- @export
export const encoded = json.encoded

--- Validates and retains one exact JSON number token.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local json = serde.json
--- assert(json.encodevalue(json.numbertoken("12345678901234567890")) == "12345678901234567890")
--- ```
--- @param text the exact numeric lexeme
--- @return a validated fragment for a JSON writer or encoder
--- @raises string when text is not one JSON number token
--- @export
export const numbertoken = json.numberToken

--- Encodes and interns a string for trusted use as either a key or value.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local key = serde.json.encodedstring("user_id")
--- ```
--- @param value the unencoded string
--- @return its reusable encoded representation
--- @export
export const encodedstring = json.encodedString

--- Validates existing JSON bytes once without decoding or re-encoding them.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local cached = serde.json.verified([[{"id":41}]])
--- ```
--- @param text a complete JSON value
--- @return a handle retaining the original immutable string
--- @export
export const verified = json.verified

--- Validates and interns existing bytes containing a complete JSON string.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local key = serde.json.verifiedstring([["user_id"]])
--- ```
--- @param text an encoded JSON string, including its quotes
--- @return a handle retaining the original immutable string
--- @export
export const verifiedstring = json.verifiedString

--- Creates a schema-driven JSON codec with an immutable wire profile.
--- Sentinel that decodes from and encodes as JSON null.
--- @export
export const NULL = json.NULL

--- An explicit empty JSON array.
--- @export
export const EMPTY_ARRAY = json.EMPTY_ARRAY

--- An explicit empty JSON object.
--- @export
export const EMPTY_OBJECT = json.EMPTY_OBJECT
